 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-1029) '|         | developers and (C) 2001-2005 CG Group, RWTH Aachen       |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Geogram | BSD 3-Clause, (C) 2000-2022 Inria                        |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-1029) 'Both clear RFD66606-1028's gate. PMP's variant is MIT text with an added employer disclaimer, so it is recorded as that and not as plain MIT.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-1029) 'None of the candidates in this section carries blend shapes, and on an avatar that is the binding constraint rather than a detail.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The tri-to-quad step is ported, not adopted
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The Optimized-Tris-to-Quads-Converter is a Blender add-on, and Blender is blocklisted with no exemption. Its algorithm needs no host: an interior edge shared by exactly two triangles is a candidate to dissolve, each triangle may be dissolved at most once, and the goal is to dissolve as many as possible, which is a matching on the dual graph. It now lives in `character-fox/Tools/rig/tris_to_quads.py`, with the PuLP formulation carried across unchanged and an exact matching solver beside it as a cross-check.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The add-on delegated the dissolve to its host, and that is the part with teeth. Pairs are refused before the solver sees them when the two triangles disagree on facing, fold past 40 degrees, would merge into a concave quad, or would leave a sliver corner. A bent or concave quad is triangulated back along the other diagonal, so the silhouette moves and nothing reports it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-1029) '`jp.lilxyzw.ndmfmeshsimplifier` is blocklisted separately. It is a capable quadric decimator that preserves shapes, but it runs at build time, so the mesh that ships is not the mesh in the scene.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The meshes are quad-derived, and what that is worth
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-1029) 'Running the ported converter on real geometry, 2026-09-12: the Dress's 6,408 triangles pair into 3,204 quads with NOTHING left over, 100.0% coverage. Dress_shoulder reaches 99.4% and Dress_Ribbon 95.3%.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-1029) 'A perfect pairing does not happen by luck. It means the mesh was authored as quads and triangulated on export, so the original edge flow is intact and recoverable. That is the same property RFD66606-2029 wants from a curvenet: a bounded, inspectable graph rather than a soup.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-1029) '**Recovering quads does not by itself save a triangle.** The platform counts triangles and a quad is two of them, so 6,408 in is 6,408 out. The value is that a quad mesh with intact flow supports removing a whole edge loop, which halves resolution in one direction and preserves the flow, where quadric decimation picks vertices by error and leaves flow to chance. On a garment, whose silhouette IS its edge flow, that difference is the point.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The curvenet idea applies in this adapted form rather than directly. CASSIE authors a curve network from strokes; it does not reverse-engineer one from a dense mesh, and the capability inventory records its patch surface creation as losing about 90%. For an existing mesh the practical form of the same idea is field-guided retopology at a chosen budget, which Instant Meshes already provides under BSD-3.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-1029) 'What the blend shapes on this avatar actually look like
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-1029) 'Measured 2026-09-12 on Miroir-Re: 647 shapes across ten meshes. Two structural facts make this the easiest possible transfer case. Every shape is single-frame, so there are no in-betweens to interpolate, and not one carries normal or tangent deltas, so only positions move.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:38Z (RFD66606-1029) 'Individual shapes are tightly localised. Body's 448 shapes move a median 5.10% of its vertices; BaseBody's 31 move 2.06%; the debug mesh's 113 move 0.91%, which on 440 vertices is four vertices. Ear and Tail are the exception at 100%, being whole-mesh squashes.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:39Z (RFD66606-1029) 'That suggests a strategy: protect the vertices a shape touches and decimate the rest. It does not survive the union.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:40Z (RFD66606-1029) '| mesh        | verts | shapes | moved by ANY shape | free  |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:41Z (RFD66606-1029) '| ----------- | ----- | ------ | ------------------ | ----- |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:42Z (RFD66606-1029) '| BaseBody    |  9660 |     31 |              99.8% |  0.2% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:43Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Body        |  7065 |    448 |              92.9% |  7.1% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:44Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Ear         |  1536 |      6 |             100.0% |  0.0% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:45Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Hairband    |   931 |      8 |             100.0% |  0.0% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:46Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Tail        |  1902 |      8 |              98.4% |  1.6% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:47Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Hair        |  7737 |     27 |              54.4% | 45.6% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:48Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Dress_frill |  2755 |      1 |              11.9% | 88.1% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:49Z (RFD66606-1029) '| Cardigan    |  5793 |      1 |               2.2% | 97.8% |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:50Z (RFD66606-1029) 'Four hundred shapes at five percent each, spread over the mesh, cover it. Protecting the shaped vertices means protecting everything.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:51Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The conclusion is to avoid the transfer, not to write it
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:52Z (RFD66606-1029) 'Cardigan and Dress_frill carry one shape each and are 97.8% and 88.1% untouched, which makes them decimatable as if they were shape-free. Adding them to the seven genuinely shape-free meshes gives 31,834 triangles to work with against a deficit of 8,154, a 25.6% reduction, where the shape-free meshes alone needed 47.2%. Hair adds another 8,944 at 45.6% free if that is not enough.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:53Z (RFD66606-1029) 'So the meshes whose shapes cover them can be left alone. Those are the face tracking meshes, where a shape moving four vertices is exactly what decimation removes first.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:54Z (RFD66606-1029) 'If a transfer is ever needed, the trap is recorded here. A shape that moves four vertices is invisible in any rest-pose comparison, because at weight zero the mesh is correct. Verification has to evaluate each shape at weight one and compare the deformed surfaces, one shape at a time, or a destroyed expression ships looking fine.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:55Z (RFD66606-1029) 'PartPacker replacement (image to raw mesh)
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:56Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The catalog already carries TRELLIS.2 and TRELLIS. Both use the MIT license. Prefer them over PartPacker.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:57Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:58Z (RFD66606-1029) 'The PartField, FastMesh, and PartPacker tasks have no model until each gets a replacement.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1029  1 LINE
 Text:Written
